1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,966
A spoon of plain rice.

2
00:00:01,966 --> 00:00:03,436
Chew it for a minute.

3
00:00:03,436 --> 00:00:05,604
Slowly, it starts to taste sweet.

4
00:00:06,004 --> 00:00:07,085
Nothing was added.

5
00:00:07,085 --> 00:00:09,296
Something in your mouth changed the food.

6
00:00:09,746 --> 00:00:11,369
Diya noticed this at lunch.

7
00:00:11,369 --> 00:00:15,060
She asked: how can starch break
down so fast at body temperature?

8
00:00:15,060 --> 00:00:16,735
And what is doing the work?

9
00:00:17,085 --> 00:00:18,633
Here is the short answer.

10
00:00:18,633 --> 00:00:20,036
Proteins called enzymes.

11
00:00:20,036 --> 00:00:24,114
Each has a small pocket that grabs a
molecule and speeds up its reaction.

12
00:00:24,614 --> 00:00:28,895
By the end, you will know what an enzyme
is, its active site, the catalytic

13
00:00:28,895 --> 00:00:31,533
cycle, and how enzymes
lower the energy hill.

14
00:00:32,183 --> 00:00:33,971
First, what is an enzyme?

15
00:00:34,322 --> 00:00:35,811
Almost all enzymes are proteins.

16
00:00:35,811 --> 00:00:37,953
A few are nucleic acids
that act like enzymes.

17
00:00:37,953 --> 00:00:39,228
These are called ribozymes.

18
00:00:39,678 --> 00:00:42,052
An enzyme's chain folds into a 3D shape.

19
00:00:42,052 --> 00:00:45,277
As it folds, it criss-crosses
itself and leaves pockets.

20
00:00:45,277 --> 00:00:46,686
One pocket is special.

21
00:00:46,686 --> 00:00:48,148
It is the active site.

22
00:00:48,798 --> 00:00:51,125
Let's zoom into an enzyme at work.

23
00:00:51,475 --> 00:00:56,082
Inside a cell, thousands of types of
enzymes work, each on its own reaction.

24
00:00:56,582 --> 00:00:57,560
Zoom closer.

25
00:00:57,560 --> 00:01:01,712
One enzyme is a folded protein,
with a pocket on its surface.

26
00:01:02,212 --> 00:01:03,567
Zoom to the pocket.

27
00:01:03,567 --> 00:01:08,213
The active site is a crevice that fits
one kind of molecule, the substrate.

28
00:01:08,213 --> 00:01:11,158
The substrate is the
chemical that gets changed.

29
00:01:11,808 --> 00:01:15,216
Now follow one catalytic
cycle, step by step.

30
00:01:15,566 --> 00:01:16,466
Step one.

31
00:01:16,290 --> 00:01:19,565
The substrate binds to the
active site and fits into it.

32
00:01:20,315 --> 00:01:21,215
Step two.

33
00:01:21,017 --> 00:01:24,893
This binding makes the enzyme change
shape, so it fits even more tightly

34
00:01:24,893 --> 00:01:26,159
around the substrate.

35
00:01:26,909 --> 00:01:27,809
Step three.

36
00:01:27,808 --> 00:01:31,519
The active site breaks the
chemical bonds of the substrate.

37
00:01:31,519 --> 00:01:33,512
An enzyme-product complex forms.

38
00:01:34,262 --> 00:01:35,162
Step four.

39
00:01:34,783 --> 00:01:36,102
The enzyme releases the products.

40
00:01:36,852 --> 00:01:37,752
Step five.

41
00:01:37,657 --> 00:01:42,237
The free enzyme is ready to bind the
next substrate and run the cycle again.

42
00:01:42,987 --> 00:01:43,976
Remember this.

43
00:01:43,976 --> 00:01:45,742
The enzyme is not used up.

44
00:01:46,342 --> 00:01:49,201
Now, why does this make the reaction fast?

45
00:01:49,201 --> 00:01:50,859
Look at an energy hill.

46
00:01:51,209 --> 00:01:55,407
To become product, the substrate must
first climb to a high-energy shape

47
00:01:55,407 --> 00:01:57,202
called the transition state.

48
00:01:57,202 --> 00:02:00,326
The height of this climb
is the activation energy.

49
00:02:00,776 --> 00:02:03,597
An enzyme brings down this energy barrier.

50
00:02:03,597 --> 00:02:08,565
The climb is lower, so the substrate
changes into product much more easily.

51
00:02:09,215 --> 00:02:11,253
Enzymes are catalysts, things
that speed up reactions.

52
00:02:11,253 --> 00:02:12,789
But they differ from inorganic catalysts.

53
00:02:13,139 --> 00:02:16,627
Inorganic catalysts work best at
high temperature and high pressure.

54
00:02:17,077 --> 00:02:20,922
Enzymes get damaged at high temperatures,
say above forty degrees Celsius.

55
00:02:20,922 --> 00:02:24,750
But enzymes from organisms in hot springs
stay stable up to eighty to ninety

56
00:02:24,750 --> 00:02:25,650
degrees.

57
00:02:25,980 --> 00:02:27,366
Let's measure speed.

58
00:02:27,716 --> 00:02:32,145
The rate of a reaction is the amount
of product formed per unit time.

59
00:02:32,145 --> 00:02:36,779
As a rule of thumb, the rate doubles
or halves for every ten degree change

60
00:02:36,779 --> 00:02:37,872
in temperature.

61
00:02:38,322 --> 00:02:40,551
Many reactions are steps in a chain.

62
00:02:40,551 --> 00:02:44,740
When each step is run by an enzyme,
the chain is a metabolic pathway.

63
00:02:44,740 --> 00:02:47,836
Glucose becomes pyruvic acid
through ten enzyme steps.

64
00:02:48,436 --> 00:02:49,336
Your turn.

65
00:02:49,287 --> 00:02:52,956
Does an enzyme raise the
activation energy, or lower it?

66
00:02:52,956 --> 00:02:54,208
Pause and decide.

67
00:02:57,408 --> 00:02:58,400
It lowers it!

68
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:01,376
A lower energy hill lets
the reaction run faster.

69
00:03:02,026 --> 00:03:03,640
Now, a worked example.

70
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:05,951
How much faster is one real enzyme?

71
00:03:06,301 --> 00:03:09,148
Carbon dioxide and water
make carbonic acid.

72
00:03:09,148 --> 00:03:13,125
With no enzyme, about two hundred
molecules form in an hour.

73
00:03:13,575 --> 00:03:17,491
With the enzyme carbonic anhydrase,
about six hundred thousand molecules

74
00:03:17,491 --> 00:03:18,688
form every second.

75
00:03:18,688 --> 00:03:20,965
That is over two billion in an hour.

76
00:03:21,415 --> 00:03:23,726
Divide two billion by two hundred.

77
00:03:23,726 --> 00:03:27,479
The enzyme speeds up the
reaction about ten million times.

78
00:03:28,129 --> 00:03:29,591
Now, the exam trap.

79
00:03:29,941 --> 00:03:33,146
Many students think the enzyme
is used up in the reaction.

80
00:03:33,546 --> 00:03:37,199
But at the end of the cycle the
enzyme is released unchanged.

81
00:03:37,199 --> 00:03:39,571
Only the substrate changes into product.

82
00:03:40,221 --> 00:03:41,461
Where is this asked?

83
00:03:41,461 --> 00:03:45,123
As statement questions on the active
site, ribozymes, activation energy

84
00:03:45,123 --> 00:03:46,530
and the catalytic cycle.

85
00:03:47,130 --> 00:03:48,280
Exam level now.

86
00:03:48,280 --> 00:03:49,977
Read the four statements.

87
00:03:49,977 --> 00:03:51,176
Pause and solve.

88
00:03:56,576 --> 00:03:57,785
The answer is C.

89
00:03:57,785 --> 00:04:00,429
Statements one, two and four are correct.

90
00:04:00,929 --> 00:04:03,605
Almost all enzymes are proteins, and
ribozymes are nucleic acids that act

91
00:04:03,605 --> 00:04:04,505
as enzymes.

92
00:04:04,650 --> 00:04:07,307
The enzyme-substrate
complex is short-lived.

93
00:04:07,307 --> 00:04:11,519
But enzymes lower activation
energy, so statement three is wrong.

94
00:04:12,119 --> 00:04:13,659
Here is your recap.

95
00:04:13,659 --> 00:04:15,242
Screenshot this card.

96
00:04:18,442 --> 00:04:20,628
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

97
00:04:21,028 --> 00:04:21,928
One.

98
00:04:21,463 --> 00:04:22,911
What is an active site?

99
00:04:23,811 --> 00:04:24,711
Two.

100
00:04:24,072 --> 00:04:24,972
What are ribozymes?

101
00:04:25,678 --> 00:04:26,578
Three.

102
00:04:26,257 --> 00:04:27,956
What is activation energy?

103
00:04:29,056 --> 00:04:33,854
Next time: why does an enzyme stop
working when it is too hot, too cold,

104
00:04:33,854 --> 00:04:35,684
or blocked by a look-alike?

105
00:04:35,684 --> 00:04:38,830
We find out in factors
affecting enzyme activity.
